DEBUG    | 2026-09-22 13:44:16 | course_creator:run_adk_curriculum_for_document:170 | Event from ResearchAgent: model_version='gemini-3.5-flash-lite' content=Content(
  parts=[
    Part(
      text="""{
    "Linear Algebra Foundations": "Covers foundational linear algebra topics useful for machine learning, including Gram-Schmidt orthogonalization (constructing orthogonal vectors u1 = a1 and u2 = a2 - ((u1^T a2)/(u1^T u1))u1, and generalizing to k vectors to form an orthogonal basis for the span of a set of linearly independent vectors), linear transformations, eigenvalue decompositions, trace and determinants, eigenvalue decomposition for symmetric matrices, and the power method.",
    "Optimization Techniques": "Focuses on mathematical optimization concepts for machine learning objectives, including gradients of vector-valued functions, Newton's method for finding roots or extrema, gradients of matrix-valued functions, gradients of log-determinants, and descent directions for matrix-valued functions.",
    "Directed Graphical Models": "Covers probabilistic directed graphical models (Bayesian networks), including directed graph concepts, canonical connections, ordered and local Markov properties, d-separation, alternative characterizations of conditional independencies, hidden Markov models, and specific case studies like the chest clinic example.",
    "Undirected Graphical Models": "Examines Markov random fields and undirected graphical models, covering Gibbs distributions visualised via undirected graphs, factorisation properties, independencies, factorisation from Markov blankets, pairwise potentials, and restricted Boltzmann machines (RBMs).",
    "Factor Graphs and Message Passing": "Deals with factor graphs and exact/approximate inference algorithms, including converting directed/undirected graphs into factor graphs, sum-product message passing, max-sum message passing (and associated backtracking tables), and the choice of variable elimination order.",
    "Inference and Learning Methods": "Covers parameter estimation, probabilistic inference, sampling, and variational techniques. Topics include maximum likelihood estimation (MLE) for Gaussians and fully observed discrete directed models, Bayesian inference for Bernoulli models and Gaussians, factor analysis, independent component analysis (ICA), score matching for exponential families, Monte Carlo methods (importance sampling, inverse transform sampling, rejection sampling, MCMC, Metropolis-Hastings convergence), and variational inference (mean-field variational approximations)."
}"""
    ),
  ],
  role='model'
